# ---------------------------------
#   ports/buildonly — ports a build needs and a running system does not
# ---------------------------------
#
#   <port>...
#
# `depends =` names a port's build and run-time needs alike. The image's
# closure (ports/base, script/lib/imageset.py) does not follow a dependency
# into a port listed here, so a compiler a recipe builds with stays off the
# medium. A listed port is still built, still in the binhost and still
# installed by `kdos update install` or a source build that needs it, and
# ports/base naming one puts it back on the medium.
#
# A PORT HERE MUST BE NOTHING A PROGRAM ON THE IMAGE RUNS OR LOADS. 087_gates
# proves it: linkcheck fails the image when a DT_NEEDED or a #! resolves to
# nothing left in it, and `imageset.py --verify` when a kept package's
# recorded run-time needs (`kpkg needs`: its sonames, interpreters, QML and
# Python imports and `runtime =` words) are met only by a port the image
# drops. A package installed before needs were recorded is not measured by
# the second, so `kpkg needs --scan` over the build tree is what a new entry
# is checked with.
# llvm is not listed: Mesa loads libLLVM. gcc is not: its package carries
# libstdc++ and libgcc_s. libtool is not: it carries libltdl. A port that
# does not exist fails preflight. A line starting with '#' and a blank line
# are skipped.

# Compilers and their bootstraps. Mesa builds its own OpenCL C kernels for
# the Intel and NVIDIA drivers with clang, libclc and the SPIR-V translator
# and loads none of them; mesa-opencl, which loads them, is not in the base.
lldb rust go ghc ocaml zig nodejs clang libclc spirv-llvm-translator
binutils

